#---
# purpose: "an empty String pattern matches at every grapheme boundary, checked against Elixir"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # string_empty_pattern
#
# A zero-width string pattern still matches. It occurs before the first
# grapheme, between adjacent graphemes, and after the last one. That single
# rule owns the related String behavior:
#
#   * `contains?/2` sees a match even in an empty subject;
#   * `index_of/2`, fz's byte-offset search helper, reports its first match at 0;
#   * `count/2` sees one more boundary than there are graphemes;
#   * `split/2` keeps the text between every pair of boundaries; and
#   * `replace/3`, built from `split/2`, inserts at each boundary.
#
# fz's documented grapheme seam is one codepoint, not UAX #29. These examples
# therefore include ASCII, empty, multibyte, and non-Latin subjects without a
# combining sequence: they are exactly in the part of the contract fz and
# Elixir share today.
fn main() do
  assert(String.index_of("hello", "") == 0, "the first zero-width match is at byte offset 0")
  assert(String.index_of("", "") == 0, "the empty subject has one zero-width match")

  dbg(String.contains?("hello", ""))
  dbg(String.contains?("", ""))
  dbg(String.count("hello", ""))
  dbg(String.count("", ""))
  dbg(String.count("é", ""))
  dbg(String.split("hello", ""))
  dbg(String.split("", ""))
  dbg(String.split("é", ""))
  dbg(String.split("日本", ""))
  dbg(String.replace("hello", "", "-"))
  dbg(String.replace("", "", "-"))
  dbg(String.replace("é", "", "-"))
  dbg(String.replace("hello", "", ""))
end
